    @section avroexceptions Exceptions

    |!Error Code|!Raised When
    |\c AVRO-SCHEMA-ERROR|the schema is not valid Avro
    |\c AVRO-DECODE-ERROR|the data do not match the schema, or are truncated
    |\c AVRO-ENCODE-ERROR|the value cannot be represented by the schema
    |\c AVRO-RESOLUTION-ERROR|the reader and writer schemas cannot be reconciled
    |\c AVRO-CODEC-ERROR|an unsupported or corrupt container-file codec
    |\c AVRO-FILE-ERROR|container-file framing is invalid

    @section avrolimits Limits

    Values chosen so that hostile or corrupt input cannot exhaust the process:

    |!Limit|!Value|!Applies To
    |maximum nesting depth|256|schemas and data alike; a recursive schema lets the *data* drive \
        recursion depth, so this is enforced in all builds, not only under sandboxing
    |maximum decimal precision|1000|schema \c precision attribute; conversion between base 256 \
        and base 10 is quadratic in the digit count
    |maximum container block size|256 MiB|both the stored block size and the size a compressed \
        block expands to
    |maximum elements of a zero-width type|1000000|an \c array or \c map whose element type can \
        encode to zero bytes; for every other element type the block count is bounded by the \
        remaining input

    @section avrosandbox Sandboxing and Cancellation

    - @ref Qore::Avro::AvroFileReader::constructor(string) and
      @ref Qore::Avro::AvroFileWriter "AvroFileWriter::constructor(string, AvroSchema, *hash)"
      are tagged
      with the @ref Qore::PO_NO_FILESYSTEM "FILESYSTEM" functional domain; the \c binary and
      stream forms are not, so they remain usable in a filesystem-restricted
      @ref Qore::Program "Program"
    - every unbounded encode and decode loop is a cancellation point, so a long operation can be
      interrupted with @ref Qore::cancel_thread() or a
      @ref Qore::SandboxManager "SandboxManager" interrupt

